title The reading one short -- a control that named a machine, and a shelf guard blind to sequence
prompt the baton, unattended -- bakery lap 3991, rota row 1
rota lap 3991, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. Law and boundary, and the sense is touch, which only works at a surface: you cannot touch a thing's middle, you meet it where it ends. Single-Stranded says you test whether a part stands free BY TRYING TO PULL IT OUT. Both of this lap's faults were found exactly that way -- a hand run along a fence line, finding one post loose out of four.
think Opened the cold roster pass first and held still while it ran, reading only. It answered THREE reds where the card's live front names two. commit_message_guard and agent_jail are the two every ship sees; fleet_roster was the third, and it stood on this tree alone. That gap between what the card said and what the pass said was the whole lap.
think Reds-first, so I took fleet_roster before the constructive work. One leg of 38 failed -- berthed_refuses_by_tree=no -- and the diagnosis was four lines above it, in the file's own comment: three berthed legs had been moved into a pen on 20260905 precisely because they read the real fleet, and the fourth was left reading it.
think Proved the diagnosis rather than accepting it. bakery is status live, tree grain-bakery, and the loop is running in /home/keeper/grain-bakery, so it correctly does NOT refuse -- it prints the lap command. The leg wanted a refusal that can never arrive here.
think Reconsidered my first fix and made it smaller. I had written a pen-driven replacement pair before reading the whole file; reading it showed lines 95-98 ALREADY derive an away seat from the roster and prove the tree refusal. The broken leg was a duplicate written the wrong way round, so the honest move was to cut it and add the one reading it uniquely carried -- that the refusal names WHICH tree -- beside its sibling.
think Proved the new leg bites, then proved it is not a duplicate. Planted the loop's message down to a bare refusing: wrong_tree_refused STAYED yes while refusal_names_the_wanted_tree went no. Two readings, not one wearing two names. Restored the loop and checked byte-identical against the index.
think Then took %440's booked loom, which is the shelf-order guard. Measured before building: the open shelf carried ONE rise at 0a36c98a9 when I started and TWO by the time I fetched, because a peer's own row had landed misordered in the commit that closed %431. Three hand-repairs of one file in one evening.
think Chose document order over a sort, and integers over strings. The stamps are compared in the order the page carries them; the dot is stripped so a stamp is one fourteen-digit integer, far inside a double's exact range, rather than trusting awk to decide whether a field looked numeric.
obs THE CLOSED-SHELF FREEDOM NEEDED NO EXEMPTION, and that is the Air row's lesson in one line. A closed shelf never enters $PINS, so the new gate inherited accrete-never-break from where it stands. I pulled on the part and nothing else came with it.
obs THE CONTROL'S OWN FIXTURES WERE ASCENDING. Three plants wrote 100000, 100001, 100002 down the page -- a shape no room in this tree carries -- which asserted nothing and would have reddened under the new gate. Flipping them changes no reading they make, and it means the fixture now models the page it is a fixture for.
obs A TIE IS THE DUPLICATE READING, NEVER THIS ONE. Two rows sharing a stamp already red as duplicates, so the order reading reads past an equal pair. One fault, counted once, rather than two readings both firing and a reader having to work out they are the same thing.
obs I DID NOT TOUCH THE SHELF. I had sorted it -- 53 rows, 6 lines moved, every byte preserved -- and then dropped that edit to fast-forward past two peer commits, and the merged shelf read rows_misordered=0 because a third hand had sorted it upstream while I worked. So the guard ships against a clean live reading with no repair riding along, which is the better shape.
obs THE FLEET SPINE MOVED TWICE UNDER ME. xy went e8be41ead -> 0a36c98a9 -> ad0014bc7 during the lap, and next_free went 445 -> 446 as a peer booked the number I had read. Fetch-before-book did its job both times; I read the spine again rather than trusting the first answer.
obs TWO STALE CLAIMS ON THE CARD, both from the same renumbering storm. %431 was still described as OPEN on a fourth tree after a peer closed it, and the topology doc row was cited as %440 when the ledger has it at %439 -- %440 is the shelf-order row. Corrected in the same touch, since a reader following either lands somewhere the sentence never meant.
obs THE CARD PAID FOR ITSELF. It stood at 32,598 of 32,768, so the entry had to earn its bytes: the two stale claims and one duplicated agent_jail passage funded it, and the pin closes at 32,761 with 7 to spare.
obs THE SPINE COLLIDED AND THE FOLD DISSOLVED, both on the final rebase. A peer had booked %446 at 20260905.230021 -- an earlier stamp, published first -- so a published number never moves and mine derived to %447 -- then a THIRD peer published %447 at 20260905.233110, earlier again, and mine derived to %448. Three renumbers in one send, each one the guard telling the truth. The same rebase brought their fold of %439, %440 and %441 onto their own shelf, which meant my shelf held %440 twice; and upstream's pin had dropped to 16,215 bytes, so my 2,696-byte row fits on the pin and NO fold was needed at all. Deleted my shelf, seated %447 on the pin, and appended %440's closure clause to the peer's shelf where the row now lives.
obs MY NEXT DOOR WAS TAKEN WHILE I WORKED. agent_jail is a peer's %446 now, booked at 20260905.230021 with a sharper diagnosis than mine -- NoNewPrivs 1 / Seccomp 2 in the lap's own process, so the sandbox refuses bwrap's mount namespace. I pointed the card's line at their row rather than keeping a claim on it.
obs THE HOT PASS READ ONE RED WHERE THE COLD READ THREE. fleet_roster is green because this lap closed it; commit_message_guard is green because a peer landed publish-seed.sh as a tracked file mid-lap and %431 with it. What is left is agent_jail, and it is an environment fact rather than a tree red -- a control that builds a sandbox cannot run from inside one. 111 green, 1 red, 4 gated.
loom witness=fleet_roster_witness control_checks=38 control_failures=0 wall_s=3
